  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nShowers and a few embedded thunderstorms across southwest Wyoming \nare being supported by a shortwave disturbance early this Tuesday \nmorning. This shortwave and the associated showers and thunderstorms \nwill shift east across the Green River Basin and Sweetwater County \nand fizzle out by sunrise. This shortwave is rounding an upper level \nhigh that is centered over New Mexico and Texas.\n\nToday, this upper high will build north into the Great Basin, with \nit's ridge axis extending into Idaho, placing Wyoming in west-\nnorthwest flow aloft. An embedded disturbance will traverse across \nthe state and provide support for showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening. Shower and thunderstorm coverage is forecast \nto be less widespread compared to Monday and be largely \norographically and diurnally driven along and east of the \nContinental Divide. Available moisture will be greatest across the \naforementioned areas thanks to the west-northwest flow aloft and an \nassociated boundary dropping into northern and central Wyoming. \nThunderstorms are expected today with lifted indices ranging from \n-1C to -3C and BLCAPE values around 500 J/kg; however, height rises \naloft will hinder severe thunderstorm potential. That being said, a \nfew showers or thunderstorms will be capable of gusty outflows up to \n45 mph. Showers and thunderstorms are forecast to develop between \nnoon and 2PM across the higher terrain along the Continental Divide \nand shift east-southeast through the afternoon. Shower and \nthunderstorms largely come to an end by 9PM tonight, with a few \nlight showers possible across northwest Wyoming until 11PM. With \n700mb temperatures around 10C, anticipate high temperatures in the \nupper 70s and 80s.\n\nThe upper level ridge is forecast to begin a slow shift east across \nWyoming early Wednesday and be east of Wyoming by Thursday evening. \nThe ridge axis is currently forecast to be over western Wyoming on \nWednesday as another weak disturbance propagates around it. Although \nthere will be some support and available moisture aloft, height \nrises will keep shower and thunderstorm activity orographically and \ndiurnally driven across southwest Wyoming and along and east of the \nContinental Divide. On Thursday, the ridge axis shifts east across \nWyoming and places western Wyoming in west-southwest flow by peak \nheating. This west-southwest flow will funnel Pacific moisture into \nthe area and provide support for diurnal shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment across west and northwest Wyoming. Because the ridge is \nover Wyoming on both Wednesday and Thursday, temperatures are \nexpected to warm into 80s west of the Divide and mid 80s to mid 90s \neast of the Divide both afternoons.\n\nEnsemble guidance is in consensus that the Intermountain West will \nbe positioned between the ridge in the central CONUS and an \napproaching Pacific Northwest trough on Friday. This places Wyoming \nin southwest flow with Pacific moisture funneling into the region. \nThis looks to be the pattern Friday through at least Sunday morning \nas the Pacific Northwest trough shifts east. The specific details on \ntiming, precipitation amounts, and coverage are unknown at this time, \nhowever, confidence in an active weather weekend is increasing.",
